        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "1. What social stratification is",
        "tekst": "**The Titanic** (1912): of more than 1,600 dead, 80% were men (women and children first), and class decided the rest: more than **60%** of first-class passengers survived, **36%** of second class and only **24%** of third class, mostly because first class was on the upper decks, closest to warnings and lifeboats. \"All animals are equal, but some are more equal than others\" (Orwell).\n\n**[[Social divisions]]**: human differences that are **rendered socially significant** (stamp collectors versus cooks is a difference; rich versus poor is a division). **[[Social stratification]]**: a system by which a society **ranks categories of people in a hierarchy**, giving some a disproportionate share of **money, power and prestige**.\n\n**Five principles of social stratification:**\n1. It is a **characteristic of society**, not of individual differences (first class did not survive because they swam better).\n2. It **persists over generations**: parents pass on their position. **[[Social mobility]]** (upward, downward or horizontal) exists, but most people stay roughly where they started.\n3. It is **universal but variable**: found everywhere, but what is unequal and how unequal differs.\n4. It involves **beliefs**, not only inequality: every system defines itself as fair ([[ideology|Ideology]], hegemony); the privileged support it most.\n5. It produces **shared identities**: people know their place and may accept, negotiate or resist it (Marx's [[class consciousness|Class consciousness]]).\n\n**Forms of social division**: socio-economic (class), gender and sexuality, ethnicity and race, age, and health and disability (plus language, dialect and nationality). They overlap: **[[intersectionality|Intersectionality]]**.\n\n**Iris Marion Young's five processes of inequality:**\n1. **[[Social exclusion]] and marginalisation**: a whole category is expelled from useful participation in social life.\n2. **Exploitation**: the results of one group's labour benefit another.\n3. **Powerlessness**: lacking the authority, status and sense of self that professionals have.\n4. **[[Cultural imperialism]]**: a dominant group's experience and culture become the norm.\n5. **Violence**: directed at people simply because they belong to a group.\n\n**Research in Action:** Pierre Bourdieu's *The Weight of the World* (1993/1999): about 70 in-depth interviews with people leading \"damaged lives\" through racism, poverty and marginalisation, shown side by side with people in other positions.",
