        "titel": "2. Closed and open systems: slavery, estate, caste and class",
        "tekst": "Systems differ in how **closed** (little movement) or **open** (some mobility) they are (Tumin).\n\n**[[Slavery]]**: people owned by others as property (**chattel slavery**). Ancient Egypt, Persia, Greece and Rome; the Atlantic slave trade brought 10-15 million Africans to the New World, with 4-6 million more dying on the way. Abolished by the British Empire in 1833 and the US in 1865. **[[Modern slavery]]** (Kevin Bales): not ownership but **control through violence** plus economic exploitation; about **27 million** people (other estimates up to 200 million), in four forms: **forced labour**, **[[debt bondage|Debt bondage]]** (sometimes inherited), **forced prostitution** and trafficking, and **servile marriage**. Even an estimate of 3,000 Paris households holding slaves.\n\n**[[Estate system]]**: medieval feudal Europe: a rigid hierarchy of **rights and obligations** between nobility, clergy and commoners; lords, vassals and peasants.\n\n**[[Caste system]]**: based on **ascription** (inherited status); a pure caste system is closed: birth alone decides. India's **[[varna|Varna]]**: **Brahmans** (priests, about 3%), **Kshatriyas** (warriors and rulers), **Vaishyas** (merchants and landowners), **Shudras** (artisans and servants), with the **\"untouchables\"** ([[Dalits]], \"oppressed people\") outside, about 150 million, doing \"impure\" work. Formally abolished over half a century ago, yet some 170 million people in about 450 castes; perhaps 50,000 Dalits in the UK. **Race as caste**: the US South after slavery, and **[[apartheid|Apartheid]]** South Africa.\n\n**Four traits of a caste system:** (1) caste is linked to **occupation**; (2) **[[endogamous marriage|Endogamous marriage]]** (within the same rank); (3) everyday life is **segregated** (ideas of ritual purity and pollution); (4) it rests on powerful **cultural beliefs** (accepting your work as moral duty). Caste is typical of **agrarian** societies and erodes with industrialisation, but stratification does not end; it changes form.\n\n**[[Class system]]**: stratification resulting from the unequal distribution of **wealth, power and prestige**; more open, based more on **achievement**; education, migration and urbanisation allow mobility, and equality before the law becomes central. People remain unequal, but less by the accident of birth.\n\n**[[Status consistency]]**: how consistent a person's standing is across wealth, power and prestige. High in caste systems; **lower in class systems** (a professor with high prestige but a modest income), which is why classes are less sharply defined than castes.\n\n**South Africa: from \"race as caste\" to class.** Apartheid (law from 1948) denied black South Africans citizenship, land and a vote; resistance, boycotts and Mandela's release (1990) led to the first all-race election in **1994**. A Truth and Reconciliation Commission heard over 20,000 witnesses. Yet South Africa may now be **more unequal** than under apartheid: a shift from race to **class**, between multiracial **\"insiders\"** with good jobs and **\"outsiders\"** (the 7 million ukublelekka, \"marginal people\").",
