        "antwoord": "**Partly right.** Dahrendorf gave four reasons why the revolution did not come: the capitalist class fragmented into many shareholders and a managerial class; white-collar work and a rising standard of living made workers see themselves as better off; workers organised in unions, with institutionalised labour-management negotiation; and legal protections (rights, access to the courts, social security) grew. Together these smoothed capitalism's rough edges.\n\n**But Marx's analysis is still relevant**, say his defenders, for five reasons: wealth is still highly concentrated and inequality has grown; global capitalism moved the sweatshops to low-income countries; much work remains degrading and dehumanising; labour activity has weakened, with many non-unionised workers; and the law still favours the rich. So the prediction of revolution failed in industrial societies, but the critique of inequality and exploitation still has force. (The slide adds a general critique: Marxism is revolutionary, and severing rewards from performance generates low productivity.)"
